ChatGPT Pro with account

I would build **one bot: a reference-anchored, inventory-aware market maker with an aggressive taker overlay**.

The basic idea is simple:

> **External prediction markets tell us roughly what the contract is worth. We continuously quote around that value when competitors give us enough spread, and immediately take liquidity when the competition market gets materially out of line.**

I would not spend the remaining days building an ML model, a sophisticated election forecaster, or full Avellaneda–Stoikov optimisation. The useful part of the market-making literature is the inventory-aware reservation price; recent prediction-market work specifically finds that binary settlement makes inventory management materially different, and a 2026 paper derives an especially convenient inventory adjustment directly in log-odds space. ([Department of Mathematics][1])

## The strategy

For contract \(i\), maintain four numbers:

$$
F_i = \text{our estimated true probability}
$$

$$
R_i = \text{our inventory-adjusted reservation price}
$$

$$
h_i = \text{minimum passive half-spread we require}
$$

$$
q_i = \text{current inventory}
$$

Everything the bot does follows from those four numbers.

### 1. Fair value

The strongest information source should usually be the **direct equivalent contract on external markets**, not the competition's own midpoint.

For every external venue \(v\), calculate an executable microprice:

$$
MP_v =
\frac{A_v BSize_v + B_v ASize_v}
{BSize_v + ASize_v}
$$

where \(A\) and \(B\) are best ask/bid.

That pushes the estimate toward the side with greater displayed pressure without doing anything fancy.

Don't simply average external venues. Give each one a reliability weight:

$$
w_v =
C_{\text{mapping}}
\times C_{\text{fresh}}
\times C_{\text{liquidity}}
\times C_{\text{spread}}
$$

I would combine them in **log-odds**, because movements near 5% and 50% should not be treated identically:

$$
z_v = \log\frac{MP_v}{1-MP_v}
$$

$$
z_{\text{ext}}
=
\frac{\sum_v w_v z_v}{\sum_v w_v}
$$

Then:

$$
F_{\text{ext}}=\sigma(z_{\text{ext}})
$$

Use the competition market itself only as a small secondary signal:

$$
z_F =
z_{\text{ext}}
+
0.10\,
clip(z_{\text{local-micro}}-z_{\text{ext}}, -0.20,+0.20)
+
\Delta z_{\text{related}}
$$

and

$$
F=\sigma(z_F)
$$

So a healthy direct external analogue dominates the fair value. Local order flow can nudge it but cannot drag us around.

I'd use these initial weights:

| Component                    |                                          Initial importance |
| ---------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------: |
| Direct external analogue(s)  |                                                        ~90% |
| Local competition microprice |                                                        ~10% |
| Related markets              | event-driven adjustment rather than continuous large weight |

If Polymarket says 54%, Kalshi says 55%, and the SIG book says 58%, I want my fair value around **54–55%, not 57%**.

If the external venues disagree by more than about **4 percentage points**, stop aggressively trading the discrepancy. Something may be wrong with mapping, rules, or information propagation.

---

## 2. Inventory-adjusted fair value

This is the most important piece people leave out.

If \(F=55\%\), buying indefinitely below 55% is still stupid. Eventually you have far too much event risk.

Define:

$$
x_i=\frac{q_i}{q_{max,i}}
$$

and a related-market exposure:

$$
g_i = \frac{G_i}{G_{max,i}}
$$

Then:

$$
x_i^{eff}=clip(x_i+0.5g_i,-1.5,1.5)
$$

Set:

$$
R_i=
\sigma
\left(
\logit(F_i)-\lambda x_i^{eff}
\right)
$$

with:

$$
\boxed{\lambda=0.16}
$$

initially.

At 50% fair value, being at maximum long inventory knocks your personal reservation value down from about 50% to **46%**. Being maximally short pushes it toward 54%.

This is very convenient because it remains inside \((0,1)\) and naturally makes inventory adjustments smaller in probability terms near 0 and 1. A recent binary-market treatment derives this same basic log-odds structure from utility indifference pricing. ([SSRN][2])

---

## 3. When I buy

There are two ways.

**Passive buy:** place a limit bid below \(R\), but only when the existing book gives us sufficient edge.

**Aggressive buy:** cross the ask when the competition market is clearly cheap.

Let short-horizon external volatility be:

$$
\sigma_5 = EWMA(|\Delta F|,\ 5\text{s horizon})
$$

Then set the passive minimum half-edge:

$$
h =
tick
+\frac{external\ spread}{2}
+1.25\sigma_5
+toxicity
+staleness
+disagreement
$$

Typical quiet-market result with a 1-cent tick should be roughly **1.5–2 cents**.

For taking liquidity:

$$
E_{take}=\max(3tick,\;2h)
$$

Buy immediately if:

$$
R-bestAsk > E_{take}
$$

Example:

* fair value: 58%
* inventory-adjusted reservation: 57%
* best competition ask: 52%
* take threshold: 3%

Then **buy the 52 ask immediately**.

A 5-point apparent discrepancy is far more valuable than earning another cent by patiently joining the bid.

---

## 4. When I sell

Exactly symmetric.

Take the competition bid when:

$$
bestBid-R > E_{take}
$$

If shorting isn't permitted, this becomes an inventory-reduction trade only.

For passive selling, offer above reservation value whenever the current market leaves sufficient edge.

---

## 5. Yes, I market make — but selectively

I would **not** blindly quote every market at all times.

The bot supplies liquidity only when:

* the reference is healthy;
* the local touch leaves enough expected edge;
* inventory permits it;
* external markets aren't violently moving;
* recent fills haven't shown signs we're being picked off.

This distinction matters. Empirical work on binary event-contract liquidity provision highlights that passive makers can wind up absorbing persistent directional demand and carrying meaningful terminal exposure; spread capture alone isn't enough. ([SSRN][3])

### Bid construction

Calculate:

$$
B_{max}=floorTick(R-h)
$$

Ignoring our own resting order, suppose the competition best bid is \(B\).

If:

$$
B_{max}\ge B+tick
$$

quote:

$$
B+tick
$$

If:

$$
B_{max}\ge B
$$

join:

$$
B
$$

Otherwise **do not bid**.

### Ask construction

Calculate:

$$
A_{min}=ceilTick(R+h)
$$

If:

$$
A_{min}\le A-tick
$$

quote:

$$
A-tick
$$

If:

$$
A_{min}\le A
$$

join:

$$
A
$$

Otherwise **do not offer**.

That creates a useful rule:

> Never improve the market unless the improved price still gives us our required edge.

Don't quote behind the touch merely for the sake of having an order somewhere.

---

## 6. Order sizing

I would use different sizing for passive and aggressive orders.

For passive quotes, define `BASE_QUOTE` as roughly **0.05–0.10% of bankroll in maximum-loss terms**.

Also never display more than roughly:

$$
25\%
$$

of the existing top-of-book quantity initially. We don't want to become the entire market.

For the bid:

$$
size_{bid}
=
BASE
\times
clip(1-0.75x_i,0.25,1.75)
$$

For the ask:

$$
size_{ask}
=
BASE
\times
clip(1+0.75x_i,0.25,1.75)
$$

So if we're long, our bids shrink and our asks grow.

For aggressive trades I would use **capped fractional Kelly**.

For a YES contract at price \(p\), estimated probability \(F\):

$$
n_{Kelly}/W =
\frac{F-p}{p(1-p)}
$$

Use only:

$$
\boxed{10\% \text{ Kelly}}
$$

and then cap it with market and group inventory limits.

That means strong discrepancies get meaningfully larger trades without letting a 3-point modelling mistake blow up the bot.

For example at \(p=0.50\), \(F=0.55\):

$$
n/W = \frac{0.05}{0.25}=20\%
$$

Full Kelly is enormous.

At one-tenth Kelly, you hold about 2 contracts per $100 bankroll, costing about **1% of capital**.

Much more sensible.

---

## 7. When another market moves

This is where I think a large part of the competition edge can come from.

Every external-market update triggers:

**Direct equivalent moves:** if the external fair value changes by at least **0.5 tick**, recalculate immediately. If it moves by **1 tick or more**, cancel our resting orders first and recalculate second.

Don't leave a stale 51 bid sitting there while Polymarket just dropped 54 → 49.

### Related-market shock propagation

Build a small relationship graph.

Each relationship is one of:

`EQUIVALENT`, `COMPLEMENT`, `MUTUALLY_EXCLUSIVE`, `MONOTONIC`, or `STATISTICAL`.

The first four get deterministic treatment.

For statistical relationships, estimate a simple ridge regression offline:

$$
\Delta logit(p_i)
=
\beta_{ij}\Delta logit(p_j)+\epsilon
$$

No neural network.

If market \(j\) suddenly moves:

$$
\Delta z_i^{pred}=
\beta_{ij}\Delta z_j
$$

Immediately temporarily adjust \(i\)'s fair value.

I would cap this adjustment at roughly **3 probability points** unless multiple related markets confirm it.

If the predicted move exceeds one tick, cancel/requote \(i\).

To **cross the spread using only the related-market signal**, demand more evidence:

$$
E_{take,related}\approx4-5\text{ cents}
$$

rather than 3.

So cross-market signals help us get ahead without making them equivalent to direct price evidence.

---

## 8. What happens after somebody trades with us

This is critical.

Suppose somebody hits our bid.

Do **not** instantly replace exactly the same order.

Immediately:

1. update inventory;
2. cancel the remaining orders in that market;
3. refresh external references;
4. recompute \(F,R,h\);
5. classify the fill's toxicity;
6. quote again from scratch.

Maintain a `toxicity_score` per contract.

If we buy at 52 and within two seconds external fair value falls 52 → 50, that fill was probably informed/adverse.

Update:

$$
toxicity =
0.9\,toxicity +
0.1\,I(adverse\ move)
$$

Use it to add up to roughly **3 ticks** to the required spread and reduce displayed size by as much as 75%.

Conversely, if fills repeatedly don't predict adverse reference movements, decay the penalty.

This lets the bot learn something extremely useful without an ML system:

> **Which contracts/opponents are repeatedly picking us off?**

---

## 9. Inventory limits

I would start with:

$$
q_{max,i}
=
0.5\%-1.0\%
$$

of bankroll in worst-case market risk per individual contract.

And:

$$
G_{max}
\approx 5\%
$$

for a strongly correlated election cluster/factor.

Total gross event risk:

$$
\le25\%
$$

initially.

At:

**60% of \(q_{max}\):** normal inventory skew does the work.

**80%:** stop adding on that side. Quote only normally or aggressively in the inventory-reducing direction.

**95%:** actively reduce toward ~70% when an executable price is within roughly one normal half-spread of fair value.

The exact limits should be among the first things tuned in simulation.

---

## 10. Related election markets

I would keep this surprisingly simple.

For hard relationships:

* A = NOT B → \(P(A)=1-P(B)\)
* mutually exclusive exhaustive outcomes → probabilities sum to 1
* seat threshold \(X\ge20\) cannot have lower probability than \(X\ge21\)
* exact duplicate election outcomes → same underlying value

Project raw fair values onto these constraints before quoting.

For an exhaustive race, for example:

$$
\tilde p_i=\frac{p_i}{\sum_jp_j}
$$

For ordered thresholds, use isotonic regression.

This gives us **internally coherent probabilities essentially for free**.

For fuzzier relationships such as:

> Republican Senate control ↔ individual state Senate race

do not impose an arbitrage identity.

Use the statistical shock model described above.

Also count those markets together for portfolio inventory, because five individually reasonable positions can really be one giant bet on a national wave.

